Submit judges a copy of the tree taken when it starts; later edits wait
for the next submit. A tree that fails the check or build is rejected
before the board and costs no eval. A busy or failing board is reported as
`error` with a note and costs no eval; submit again. A run that faults or
hangs on the board is an `error` that does cost an eval.

Before spending an eval, run `{{ check }}` (no arguments,
foreground, Bash timeout 600000 ms). It costs no eval and no board time
(about 30 s): it applies the evaluator's source rules, compiles your tree
for the {{ cpu }} and reports `check.findings`, `build` (compiler errors and
warnings) and `code_size`{% if toolchains %}, each keyed by compiler{% endif %}.

To see the machine code your C compiles to, run
`{{ disasm }} <function_name>` after `check`. It prints that
function's disassembly from the `check` build ({{ cpu }}, the same flags the
evaluator uses). Costs no eval. Use it to confirm the compiler emits the
{% if has_mve %}MVE {% endif %}instructions, unrolling and register use you intended.
