`submit` prints one JSON verdict and saves the same text to
`{{ results }}/NNN.json` (NNN = eval number), which you
may Read if the printed output was cut:
- `verdict`: overall result, pass only if every leg passes. One of pass, fail,
  rejected, refused, no_gain, error.
- `evals_left`: you have {{ evals }} evaluations in total. Use them carefully.
- `note`: present when the eval was not charged, and why.
- `code_size`: kernel library code bytes vs base, total and per changed
  object file, or an error when nothing was built.
{%- if toolchains %}
  Keyed by compiler; `toolchains` gives each compiler's geomean speedup
  over its legs and its code bytes added.
{%- endif %}
- `legs.<leg>`, each with:
  - `verdict`, `stage` (check, run, objects, score) and `score`.
  - `findings`: why the checker rejected the tree.
  - `failures`: what failed, by kind: `comparison_failed` (output differs
    from baseline), `regression` (a case got slower beyond its noise band),
    `prepare_regression` (the untimed setup call, e.g. weight sums, got
    slower), `family_regression` (a kernel family got slower overall),
    `timing_lost` (a case no longer reports valid cycles), `missing_case`,
    `input_digest` (inputs changed), `not_comparable`.
  - `families`: geomean speedup per kernel family over touched cases, plus
    `untouched_cases` and `untouched_geomean`.
  - `cases`: one row per public case your change touched (see
    `cases_columns`): baseline vs candidate cycles, speedup, noise band (%),
    cycles per MAC. `untouched_cases` gives only their count and speedup
    range.
  - `hints` (`{{ first_leg }}` leg only): for the ten slowest touched cases, % of peak, a
    diagnosis from PMU counters and suggested fixes.
  - `hidden`: count of unseen cases, their failures by kind, and in
    `failed` the kernel symbol and the wrapper path ("via") of each failing
    hidden case. Ids, shapes and cycle numbers are never shown.
- Only cases whose compiled kernel code you changed (directly, or through a
  function or wrapper they call) can fail on speed. Untouched cases are
  only summarized and cannot fail you; their drift comes from code layout.
  Correctness applies to every case.

Besides speed, weigh these, where they apply:
- Code size. Flash is scarce on these chips. Prefer the smallest change that
  gets the gain: no per-shape copies of a kernel, no unrolling beyond what
  pays off, share helpers. Report bytes added per % speedup.
- Memory and caching. Think about where each operand lives and how often it
  is read: reuse each loaded weight across several output pixels and each
  loaded input across several channels, keep loads contiguous, and avoid
  re-reading weights per pixel. The hints report L1D refills and memory
  stalls.
- Tiling. Block loops over output channels and pixels so the working set
  fits the {% if has_mve %}8 MVE vector registers{% else %}core registers{% endif %} and the cache, and finish tails with
  {% if has_mve %}predication{% else %}short tail loops{% endif %} rather than scalar loops.
